// Prime Number Hashing Algorithm.
float ihash(float x) {
    int n = floatBitsToInt(x);
    n *= n - 286331153;
    n *= n + 286331153;
    return float(n) / float(~0u) + 0.5;
}

//
// Explanation:
//
// For a suitable
//    int n = floatBitsToInt(x);
// value, we want to randomize its bits.
//
// Because of a property of Heegner humbers we have:
//    For all n < 16 the formula:
//       f(n) = n * (n + 1) + 17
//    yields a prime number <= 257.
//
// Now, having
//    n & 0x0F = n mod 16 (< 16)
// and
//    17 = 0x11
// ensures that
//    (n & 0x0F) * ((n & 0x0F) + 1) + 0x11
// is always a prime number <= 257. <- EDIT: mistake made 257 > 256 doesn't fit
//
// We also know that we can use k = n + 1 such that
//    n * (n + 1) = k * (k - 1)
// modulo 16, therefore:
//    (n & 0x0F) * ((n & 0x0F) - 1) + 0x11
// is always a prime number <= 257.
//
// So then we find that
//    ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) * ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) - 0x01010101) + 0x11111111)
// encodes an ivec4(a, b, c, d) with a,b,c,d all primes <= 257.
// 
// But as -1 = 0xFFFFFFFF we also find that
//    ((n & 0xF0F0F0F0) * ((n & 0xF0F0F0F0) - 0x10101010) + 0x11111111)
// encodes an ivec4(a, b, c, d) with a,b,c,d all primes <= 257 as well.
//
// The sum of the prime number vectors above will then be:
// ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) * ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F) - 0x01010101) + 0x11111111)
// +
// ((n & 0xF0F0F0F0) * ((n & 0xF0F0F0F0) - 0x10101010) + 0x11111111)
// =
// (n - 0x11111111) * n + 0x22222222.
//
// So with two rounds of this multiplicative prime number transformation:
// n -> (n - 0x11111111) * n + 0x22222222
// composed with
// n -> (n - 0x11111111) * n + 0x22222222
// =
// n -> (n - 0x11111111) * n
// composed with
// n -> (n + 0x11111111) * n + 0x22222222.
// 
// We then find that we can equivalently drop the last additive term
// because of the pediodicity of modulo operations.
//
// Now note that 0x11111111 = 286331153.
//
// This means that we can do this full prime randomization as follows:
//    n *= n - 286331153;
//    n *= n - 286331153;
// 
// So then having full integer range we can normalize this to a float
// between zero and one by taking
//    float(n) / 43e8 + 0.5.
// 
// Which yields the result we want.
